==============================================================================
(3) ATTENUATION CEILING ON THE HEADLINE r = 0.953
==============================================================================
  Split-half (odd vs even window) reliabilities of the per-subject DiDs, from
  notes/review_results/partB/splithalf_tables.md (the values the paper quotes as
  '0.71-0.74 and 0.69-0.72'):
  ts_gsr: rel(MMI-sts DiD) = 0.717, rel(r1 DiD) = 0.741
     ceiling using the raw split-half values      = 0.729   (observed 0.953 -> disattenuated 1.31)
     ceiling using Spearman-Brown full-length rel = 0.843   (observed 0.953 -> disattenuated 1.13)
  ts_demean: rel(MMI-sts DiD) = 0.686, rel(r1 DiD) = 0.712
     ceiling using the raw split-half values      = 0.699   (observed 0.958 -> disattenuated 1.37)
     ceiling using Spearman-Brown full-length rel = 0.823   (observed 0.958 -> disattenuated 1.16)
  A correlation between two *reliable* components cannot exceed sqrt(rel*rel) if the
  two measurements' errors are independent.  The observed 0.953 exceeds both ceilings,
  and disattenuates above 1.  Errors are therefore NOT independent: a material part of
  the 0.953 is estimation noise shared by two quantities read off the same 60-TR
  windows.  This is the argument the paper applies to the residual/CCS-sts pair
  (Results 4: 'most of the within-half correlation ... is estimation noise common to
  the same windows'); it is not applied to the 0.953, and no split-half of that pair
  is reported, although splithalf_tables.md holds both reliabilities.

